in a way that smaller companies just will not be able to issue those kinds of sizes.

457
00:28:10,120 --> 00:28:12,520
The credit market tends to thrive on size.

458
00:28:12,520 --> 00:28:17,480
That being said, I do think more companies are still going to adopt

459
00:28:17,480 --> 00:28:21,560
the Bitcoin treasury standard of buying Bitcoin on their balance sheet.

460
00:28:21,560 --> 00:28:25,160
This, I think, is irrelevant of trying to become the next micro strategy.

461
00:28:25,160 --> 00:28:28,120
It's just a natural way that companies are realizing

462
00:28:28,120 --> 00:28:31,560
how they might want to create some hedge with their treasury.

463
00:28:31,560 --> 00:28:33,000
And it's actually really smart.

464
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,640
I was talking to a friend of mine who actually just recently became

465
00:28:35,640 --> 00:28:37,480
the CFO of a public company.

466
00:28:37,480 --> 00:28:40,360
And I was telling him, the first thing you should really consider

467
00:28:40,360 --> 00:28:42,600
is you should buy some Bitcoin on your balance sheet.

468
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:44,840
And the reason you should do it is because even though

469
00:28:44,840 --> 00:28:46,840
you're a billion dollar market cap company,

470
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:49,880
there are people who might be short selling it for different reasons

471
00:28:49,880 --> 00:28:52,600
that has to do with secular issues with the sector you're in.

472
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:54,600
But if you own Bitcoin on your balance sheet,

473
00:28:54,600 --> 00:28:57,000
you actually become a little bit unshortable

474
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,560
because there's a little bit of that exogenous risk

475
00:28:59,560 --> 00:29:02,760
that a PM has to underwrite that they don't want to underwrite.

476
00:29:02,760 --> 00:29:04,760
They might think this business is crap,

477
00:29:04,760 --> 00:29:07,320
but I don't actually want to short something that owns Bitcoin.

478
00:29:07,320 --> 00:29:08,760
And so I'm not going to short it.

479
00:29:08,760 --> 00:29:13,560
So the EV of a CFO in creating enterprise value

480
00:29:13,560 --> 00:29:16,280
by owning just a little Bitcoin is actually strategic,

481
00:29:16,280 --> 00:29:19,480
regardless of whether you think Bitcoin is going to go up or not.

482
00:29:19,480 --> 00:29:22,520
And I cite Tesla as a great example because Tesla,

483
00:29:22,520 --> 00:29:24,760
once upon a time, was a heavily shorted name.

484
00:29:24,760 --> 00:29:27,880
And you can actually track the day that it offered

485
00:29:27,880 --> 00:29:30,440
to share that it had Bitcoin on its balance sheet,

486
00:29:30,440 --> 00:29:32,200
the short interest actually collapsed.

487
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:35,880
And it is because there are no PMs or sell-side research

488
00:29:35,880 --> 00:29:37,640
that's going to underwrite price targets

489
00:29:37,640 --> 00:29:39,320
when Bitcoin is the underlying thing

490
00:29:39,320 --> 00:29:41,080
that they're not going to be able to control.

491
00:29:41,080 --> 00:29:44,040
So if you believe most companies are going to adopt crypto

492
00:29:44,040 --> 00:29:45,880
at some level on their balance sheet,

493
00:29:45,880 --> 00:29:49,080
they're going to participate in the Bitcoin economy regardless.

494
00:29:49,080 --> 00:29:51,960
So one of the products that Bitwise is bringing to market

495
00:29:51,960 --> 00:29:54,840
is the Bitcoin Standard Corporation's ETI.

496
00:29:54,840 --> 00:29:57,240
This is really an exciting product category for us

497
00:29:57,240 --> 00:29:59,880
because much in the way we believe that indexing

498
00:29:59,880 --> 00:30:03,160
is a great way to get diversified exposure to crypto,

499
00:30:03,160 --> 00:30:06,120
we actually think that buying a basket

500
00:30:06,120 --> 00:30:09,240
of crypto-owning public companies

501
00:30:09,240 --> 00:30:12,280
is actually going to be a secular tailwind as well.

502
00:30:12,280 --> 00:30:15,320
These are companies who are embracing ideologically

503
00:30:15,320 --> 00:30:18,040
what the next generation of consumers want.

504
00:30:18,040 --> 00:30:21,240
And the ability to build off of that brand base in itself

505
00:30:21,240 --> 00:30:24,040
could be powerful regardless of whether you become

506
00:30:24,040 --> 00:30:25,720
the next MicroStrategy or not.

507
00:30:25,720 --> 00:30:27,080
And I think you've seen some of that

508
00:30:27,080 --> 00:30:29,400
with these smaller companies like a Rumble

509
00:30:29,400 --> 00:30:31,000
or Heritage Distillery.

510
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:33,480
And because of this indexing to some approach

511
00:30:33,480 --> 00:30:34,840
of these smaller cap companies

512
00:30:34,840 --> 00:30:37,000
to have more beta to Bitcoin upside capture

513
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,800
than perhaps MicroStrategy can because of its size,

514
00:30:39,800 --> 00:30:41,720
to me, that feels equally compelling

515
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:43,720
if you're especially a small retail investor

516
00:30:43,720 --> 00:30:46,520
where you're trying to, again, find that altcoin,

517
00:30:46,520 --> 00:30:48,760
that altcoin that's going to give you a 30X run-up

518
00:30:48,760 --> 00:30:49,880
rather than a 10X.

519
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:51,160
And that's going to be a Rumble

520
00:30:51,160 --> 00:30:53,160
versus a MicroStrategy calculation.

521
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:54,920
– That's really interesting.

522
00:30:54,920 --> 00:30:57,080
I hadn't thought about it on the short seller front,

523
00:30:57,080 --> 00:30:59,400
but that reminds me of what Overstock did,

524
00:30:59,400 --> 00:31:00,680
not with Bitcoin initially,

525
00:31:00,680 --> 00:31:03,560
but do you remember back in 2015, 2016,

526
00:31:03,560 --> 00:31:05,800
they had some dividend that was payable in a...

527
00:31:05,800 --> 00:31:07,240
– Was it T0?

528
00:31:07,240 --> 00:31:09,240
– It was a token format.

529
00:31:09,240 --> 00:31:10,760
T0 was doing it for them.

530
00:31:10,760 --> 00:31:12,280
And the shorts got out of the way

531
00:31:12,280 --> 00:31:14,280
because they couldn't secure a locate

532
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:16,120
on the underlying dividend stream.

533
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:17,720
And I don't know how that ended up turning out.

534
00:31:17,720 --> 00:31:19,320
That seemed a little bit of a shortcut

535
00:31:19,320 --> 00:31:20,600
from a regulatory perspective,

536
00:31:20,600 --> 00:31:21,800
but it's the same concept

537
00:31:21,800 --> 00:31:23,480
if you just put Bitcoin on the balance sheet,

538
00:31:23,480 --> 00:31:25,560
albeit it's very clearly legal.

539
00:31:25,560 --> 00:31:26,520
– Yeah.

540
00:31:26,520 --> 00:31:28,120
Essentially, it could be

541
00:31:28,120 --> 00:31:30,520
an incredible weaponization of your treasury,

542
00:31:30,520 --> 00:31:32,440
but also it can be a defensive armor.

543
00:31:32,440 --> 00:31:33,720
Bitcoin can do both,

544
00:31:33,720 --> 00:31:35,720
which is such a unique property

545
00:31:35,720 --> 00:31:37,720
for any corporate treasurer to consider

546
00:31:37,720 --> 00:31:39,320
to have on their balance sheet.

547
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:41,560
– So outside of the Bitcoin market,

548
00:31:41,560 --> 00:31:43,400
it'll be interesting to me to see

549
00:31:43,400 --> 00:31:45,640
what type of new innovations get introduced

550
00:31:45,640 --> 00:31:47,480
once you have a market structure bill.

551
00:31:47,480 --> 00:31:48,920
If you think about the ideas

552
00:31:48,920 --> 00:31:50,040
that we've all been pitched

553
00:31:50,040 --> 00:31:51,480
over the last few years,

554
00:31:51,480 --> 00:31:54,520
a lot of them are really interesting technological ideas

555
00:31:54,520 --> 00:31:57,400
that look like they would have to be securities,

556
00:31:57,400 --> 00:31:59,640
at least for some time, to actually work.

557
00:31:59,640 --> 00:32:01,080
They'd have to raise capital.

558
00:32:01,080 --> 00:32:02,760
Some of these things have concepts

559
00:32:02,760 --> 00:32:04,520
of revenue shares built in

560
00:32:04,520 --> 00:32:06,600
that probably would trip how we test

561
00:32:06,600 --> 00:32:07,720
if that's still the framework

562
00:32:07,720 --> 00:32:09,240
that we're going to end up using here.

563
00:32:09,240 --> 00:32:10,920
But I would argue that there's been

564
00:32:10,920 --> 00:32:12,760
a lack of innovation

565
00:32:12,760 --> 00:32:15,000
for regulatory reasons on the fringes here.

566
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:16,200
And I don't know if that means

567
00:32:16,200 --> 00:32:17,720
that these things end up being

568
00:32:17,720 --> 00:32:18,760
security tokens

569
00:32:18,760 --> 00:32:20,520
or if they end up being commodities

570
00:32:20,520 --> 00:32:21,560
at some point in the future.

571
00:32:21,560 --> 00:32:23,320
But it's clear to me that there are

572
00:32:23,320 --> 00:32:25,400
a lot more ideas than there are companies

573
00:32:25,400 --> 00:32:26,680
that exist right now

574
00:32:26,680 --> 00:32:27,880
building some of these things.

575
00:32:27,880 --> 00:32:29,720
How do you think about that opportunity

576
00:32:29,720 --> 00:32:31,000
set outside of Bitcoin?

577
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:33,320
– It's a fascinating question

578
00:32:33,320 --> 00:32:36,280
and one where we have literally no precedence

579
00:32:36,280 --> 00:32:38,920
to imagine what classification

580
00:32:38,920 --> 00:32:40,520
these assets deserve.

581
00:32:40,520 --> 00:32:43,320
On one hand, even just removing

582
00:32:43,320 --> 00:32:45,160
from the lens of how we test,

583
00:32:45,160 --> 00:32:46,840
which we're all familiar with,

584
00:32:46,840 --> 00:32:48,840
is that historically commodities,

585
00:32:48,840 --> 00:32:50,040
by also definition,

586
00:32:50,760 --> 00:32:53,000
don't generate dividends.

587
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:54,680
Gold doesn't generate dividends.

588
00:32:54,680 --> 00:32:56,200
Oil doesn't generate dividends.

589
00:32:56,200 --> 00:32:57,160
Actually, cattle do

590
00:32:57,160 --> 00:32:58,200
because they give birth

591
00:32:58,200 --> 00:32:59,560
and that maybe is a dividend.

592
00:32:59,560 --> 00:33:01,080
So cattle futures maybe have

593
00:33:01,080 --> 00:33:02,840
some dividend implied in it.

594
00:33:02,840 --> 00:33:04,520
But the reality is commodities

595
00:33:04,520 --> 00:33:06,360
don't generate dividends.

596
00:33:06,360 --> 00:33:07,720
And I think that's the most challenging

597
00:33:07,720 --> 00:33:09,880
component of the security classification.

598
00:33:09,880 --> 00:33:11,240
Even moving away from the nature

599
00:33:11,240 --> 00:33:12,760
of an investment contract offering

600
00:33:12,760 --> 00:33:15,320
versus what the underlying asset represents.

601
00:33:15,320 --> 00:33:17,800
Once there's income associated

602
00:33:17,800 --> 00:33:19,080
with these assets,

603
00:33:19,080 --> 00:33:20,920
it really bridges a line

604
00:33:20,920 --> 00:33:22,680
that we just haven't explored.

605
00:33:22,680 --> 00:33:24,280
So I think that is going to be

606
00:33:24,280 --> 00:33:25,880
the big thing that the regulators

607
00:33:25,880 --> 00:33:27,800
will have to really think through,

608
00:33:27,800 --> 00:33:29,080
even away from governance,

609
00:33:29,080 --> 00:33:31,080
even away from the shared purpose

610
00:33:31,080 --> 00:33:32,680
of profit motivation.

611
00:33:32,680 --> 00:33:34,200
How do you deal with a commodity

612
00:33:34,200 --> 00:33:35,400
that distributes,

613
00:33:35,400 --> 00:33:36,040
which is something

614
00:33:36,040 --> 00:33:37,560
that just hasn't happened before?

615
00:33:37,560 --> 00:33:38,920
At the same time,

616
00:33:38,920 --> 00:33:42,200
we know that most of the investment world

617
00:33:42,200 --> 00:33:44,280
really likes distribution.

618
00:33:44,280 --> 00:33:46,040
Actually, the only way to think about

619
00:33:46,040 --> 00:33:48,520
applying any kind of valuation methodology,

620
00:33:48,520 --> 00:33:50,120
unless you're trading macro assets

621
00:33:50,120 --> 00:33:51,480
for the sake of flows,

622
00:33:51,480 --> 00:33:53,320
is trying to value

623
00:33:53,320 --> 00:33:54,840
those streams of cash flow.

624
00:33:54,840 --> 00:33:56,760
So when there's clarity around

625
00:33:56,760 --> 00:33:58,520
this being a permissible activity,

626
00:33:58,520 --> 00:34:01,080
the great unlock and jump function

627
00:34:01,080 --> 00:34:02,200
that's going to happen

628
00:34:02,200 --> 00:34:04,280
is that there's going to be new

629
00:34:04,280 --> 00:34:05,960
classes of investors

630
00:34:05,960 --> 00:34:07,960
who are going to be able to tolerate

631
00:34:07,960 --> 00:34:09,000
these assets

632
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:11,080
as part of their investment strategy.

633
00:34:11,080 --> 00:34:13,880
So I always share the example of equities

634
00:34:14,440 --> 00:34:16,440
where there's a whole journey of equities.

635
00:34:16,440 --> 00:34:18,440
So it starts with venture,

636
00:34:18,440 --> 00:34:20,040
it starts with maybe a pre-seed,

637
00:34:20,040 --> 00:34:21,880
and eventually it gets to series C,

638
00:34:21,880 --> 00:34:24,200
and maybe it goes to then a crossover fund,

639
00:34:24,200 --> 00:34:25,480
which is actually a hedge fund

640
00:34:25,480 --> 00:34:27,640
that owns private for the IPO window,

641
00:34:27,640 --> 00:34:28,440
and then it IPOs,

642
00:34:28,440 --> 00:34:30,920
and then the stock gets passed to your hedge fund

643
00:34:30,920 --> 00:34:32,040
that's trading liquid,

644
00:34:32,040 --> 00:34:34,360
and then maybe it goes into an active mutual fund,

645
00:34:34,360 --> 00:34:35,880
and then maybe it goes into the ETF one day,

646
00:34:35,880 --> 00:34:37,720
and then maybe it ends up as someone's IRA.

647
00:34:37,720 --> 00:34:40,040
And that's the journey of a stock.

648
00:34:40,040 --> 00:34:42,360
And what you see is every step along the way,

649
00:34:42,360 --> 00:34:43,720
a new investor comes in

650
00:34:43,720 --> 00:34:45,560
because the stock has matured

651
00:34:45,560 --> 00:34:48,040
for the calibration of their risk preference.

652
00:34:48,040 --> 00:34:51,080
And you need the ability to graduate assets

653
00:34:51,080 --> 00:34:53,000
along that risk preference.

654
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:54,120
The problem with crypto

655
00:34:54,120 --> 00:34:55,400
and all coins in general today

656
00:34:55,400 --> 00:34:56,840
is that there isn't a concept

657
00:34:56,840 --> 00:34:58,440
of graduating along the risk curve.

658
00:34:58,440 --> 00:35:00,120
It feels almost as if

659
00:35:00,120 --> 00:35:02,680
it's all just one contained system

660
00:35:02,680 --> 00:35:04,600
because there's no new investors

661
00:35:04,600 --> 00:35:06,200
coming in with the idea that,

662
00:35:06,200 --> 00:35:08,600
oh yeah, this has now been derisked

663
00:35:08,600 --> 00:35:09,800
because XYZ,

664
00:35:09,800 --> 00:35:10,920
and now I can own it

665
00:35:10,920 --> 00:35:13,400
at this cost of opportunity capital.

666
00:35:13,880 --> 00:35:16,920
I call this the permanent capital vehicle effect.

667
00:35:16,920 --> 00:35:19,160
Part of why the ETFs are really important

668
00:35:19,160 --> 00:35:20,200
and will continue to be

669
00:35:20,200 --> 00:35:22,920
is because ETFs bring that class of investor.

670
00:35:22,920 --> 00:35:25,000
It brings in a new investor

671
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:27,000
who is actually thinking about crypto

672
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:29,480
as a permanent capital allocation.

673
00:35:29,480 --> 00:35:31,720
And you're going to then see

674
00:35:31,720 --> 00:35:34,920
these altcoins graduate into having ETF offerings,

675
00:35:34,920 --> 00:35:36,520
hopefully with something sensible

676
00:35:36,520 --> 00:35:39,000
from the perspective of being revenue generating,

677
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:41,000
that will allow the types of investors

678
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:43,320
that we have wished upon for years

679
00:35:43,320 --> 00:35:45,640
but now there having been no regulatory clarity

680
00:35:45,640 --> 00:35:46,840
can be possible now

681
00:35:46,840 --> 00:35:47,960
than it hasn't been before.

682
00:35:48,600 --> 00:35:49,800
It's going to be really interesting

683
00:35:49,800 --> 00:35:50,840
to see how that evolves

684
00:35:50,840 --> 00:35:52,360
just from the allocator lens

685
00:35:52,360 --> 00:35:53,960
because on one hand,

686
00:35:53,960 --> 00:35:55,720
you wouldn't want to value these things

687
00:35:55,720 --> 00:35:57,800
based on cash flows if you're the team.

688
00:35:57,800 --> 00:35:59,080
The team would not want that

689
00:35:59,080 --> 00:36:00,520
because there are a lot of these things

690
00:36:00,520 --> 00:36:01,640
that are very richly valued.

691
00:36:01,640 --> 00:36:03,400
They don't have a lot of cash flows.

692
00:36:03,400 --> 00:36:04,760
So what is the TAM

693
00:36:04,760 --> 00:36:06,520
that some of these projects are going after?

694
00:36:06,520 --> 00:36:08,680
Is it non-sovereign store of value?

695
00:36:08,680 --> 00:36:10,120
Is it global internet money

696
00:36:10,120 --> 00:36:11,720
or is it something that looks like

697
00:36:11,720 --> 00:36:14,280
a commodity with cash flows associated to it?

698
00:36:14,280 --> 00:36:16,440
And I think that's still a big open question.

699
00:36:16,440 --> 00:36:18,120
A lot of these categories within crypto

700
00:36:18,120 --> 00:36:20,840
is just what is the token actually competing for?

701
00:36:20,840 --> 00:36:22,600
Just because it is money-like

702
00:36:22,600 --> 00:36:24,200
does not mean that we're going to have

703
00:36:24,200 --> 00:36:26,520
300 different internet monies.

704
00:36:26,520 --> 00:36:27,320
Absolutely.

705
00:36:27,320 --> 00:36:29,560
This is why at the core crypto

706
00:36:29,560 --> 00:36:31,080
really feels like a commodity

707
00:36:31,080 --> 00:36:32,280
more than a security today

708
00:36:32,280 --> 00:36:34,760
because it trades like a commodity.

709
00:36:34,760 --> 00:36:37,400
It trades based on market demand and supply

710
00:36:37,400 --> 00:36:40,120
in ways where the valuation

711
00:36:40,120 --> 00:36:43,400
is mostly driven by the marginal buyers and sellers.

712
00:36:43,400 --> 00:36:45,640
And that's what commodities ultimately represent.

713
00:36:45,640 --> 00:36:48,440
There's no concept of value associated with that

714
00:36:48,440 --> 00:36:49,880
in the way you're describing.

715
00:36:49,880 --> 00:36:51,720
Yeah, I think that is a big component

716
00:36:51,720 --> 00:36:54,760
of how people are going to think about assessing crypto.

717
00:36:54,760 --> 00:36:56,600
It's also why I think at some level

718
00:36:56,600 --> 00:36:58,280
Ethereum has been challenged

719
00:36:58,280 --> 00:37:00,280
and why Solana has been able to escape

720
00:37:00,280 --> 00:37:03,560
a little bit outside of that narrow valuation framework.

721
00:37:03,560 --> 00:37:05,720
Part of Ethereum's journey has always been

722
00:37:05,720 --> 00:37:09,080
that they were going to be valued like a DCF

723
00:37:09,080 --> 00:37:11,480
because of their disinflationary nature

724
00:37:11,480 --> 00:37:14,600
of the way that they were going to bring upon fee burns.

725
00:37:14,600 --> 00:37:17,480
And if you make investors subscribe to that view

726
00:37:17,480 --> 00:37:18,600
and you pound the table

727
00:37:18,600 --> 00:37:20,520
that this is the valuation methodology,

728
00:37:20,520 --> 00:37:21,720
well, you better hold them to it

729
00:37:21,720 --> 00:37:23,640
because think of it that way.

730
00:37:23,640 --> 00:37:25,240
And what you saw actually is that

731
